			<div class="content" id="post-content-2447720">
									

<p>A new understanding of the word <em>earworm</em>. Except it's a cicada, not a worm. And that personally, the picture reminds me more of wasps&#8230;! ;&gt;</p>
<p>I corrected 'are to taken' into 'are to be taken' (2nd paragraph of containment procedures).</p>
<p>Small nitpicks:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Each unit is to be constructed to resemble the human auditory system, and equipped with high-power microphones.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why the microphones, I wonder? Does the Foundation want to hear every single chant the things emit?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>At least 140 additional units are to be made available as reserve habitats for SCP-1186 instances. The maximum number of SCP-1186 instances permitted in Foundation captivity is 140;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If the maximum is 140, why 'at least' 140 additional units?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to the loss of endolymph, hosts of SCP-1186 regularly experience <span style="text-decoration: underline;">deafness and balance disorder</span>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You repeat <span style="text-decoration: underline;">this information</span> again two paragraphs later (see below), which is a bit jarring. Since you give a bit more details there, perhaps delete it here?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>in respective hosts' arterial language</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What do you mean by 'arterial language'? Just curious!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These sounds are inaudible in environments with sound levels of at least 50&#160;dB, but hosts are known to suffer from afflictions such as headaches and nausea in such environments. In 2% of known cases, non-lethal damage to hosts' lung and nervous tissues is present. Conversely, in environments with sound levels below 50&#160;dB, the severity of the aforementioned afflictions tend to subside and audibility of SCP-1186's sounds increases. Regardless of the environment, loss of hearing and balance will occur among hosts of SCP-1186.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This paragraph is a bit confusing, IMO.<br />
1) The second sentence (lung &amp; nervous tissues) feels out of place. Perhaps move it elsewhere? And BTW, why/how damage to lung and nervous tissues from a sound and consumption of endolymph?<br />
2) the description of what happens below or above 50dB is a a bit convoluted and not easy to follow. Could you perhaps reword for clarity?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Despite its age, no signs of decomposition are found; mechanism of preservation is unknown.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This reminds me of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sokushinbutsu">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sokushinbutsu</a><br />
Is this what you're alluding to? Wouldn't the Foundation know about this method of preservation and investigate?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>All this is not enough to prevent me from upvoting!</p>

							</div>

								
					
